# Flattened yamlover - multicomponent keys as path commands. Implemented in the parser and
# serializer (tools/parser: flatten.ts carries the flatten/deflatten routines); the
# normalized form is the nested one. The yed editor's flat-row support is a work in progress,
# paused - see the Status note below.
Flattening
description: multicomponent keys - a whole nested document as flat path rows, and back
- >
  *Status: the parser, serializer and file round-trips are complete. The interactive (yed)
  editor's flat-row support is still being worked out - typing and rendering the fold has
  known rough edges - and is paused for now.*
- >
  A **flat row** folds a chain of nested single-pair lines onto one line: the key chains with
  colons - the same colon grammar [paths](*::language:pointers:paths) already use - and the
  value follows the last colon:
- !!<format: text/x-yamlover> |
  key1: key2: key3: value
- >
  reads exactly as the nested spelling
- !!<format: text/x-yamlover> |
  key1:
    key2:
      key3: value
- >
  Every nested yamlover document is representable in fully flattened form and back; the
  **normalized form is the nested one**. The authored flat spelling is a REPRESENTATION, not a
  different value: every path segment after the first carries the
  [yamlover/key/flat](*::language:concretes:03-yamlover:00-yamlover_stream:00-yamlover_code:00-yamlover_structured:00-yamlover_key_value_pair:00-yamlover_key_flat)
  concrete, so the fold survives a round-trip end to end while the model stays identical -
  the same classified-and-preserved law every non-default spelling follows
  ([invariants](*::language:concretes:02-invariants)).
- >
  **Rows are commands.** Order matters in yamlover, and a sequence of flat rows is read as a
  sequence of commands that build the model by PAVING paths: each segment ensures its node
  exists (created null-valued when absent), and the value lands at the path's end. Paving is
  write-once where it must be for the reading to stay unambiguous - see the constraints below.
- >
  **The keyless segment `-`.** A path segment spelled `-` addresses keyless members - and only
  keyless members, never keyed ones. At the END of a path it CREATES: "append a new element and
  put the value there". In the MIDDLE it addresses the LAST existing keyless element and never
  adds one:
- !!<format: text/x-yamlover> |
  key1: key2: -: scalar     # append a new element under key1: key2, holding `scalar`
  key1: -: key3: 100        # in key1's LAST keyless element, pave key3: 100
- >
  Appending is also triggered by switching from the flat key to nested keying - the indented
  block under a trailing `-:` is the new element's content:
- !!<format: text/x-yamlover> |
  key1: key2: -:
        name: Alice
        age: 30
- >
  A row of scalars is therefore a repetition of trailing `-:` rows - `list: -: 1` then
  `list: -: 2` builds the two-element list. The standalone `-: value` row (the `-` as the whole
  path) is today's tolerated spelling of `- value` and keeps that reading - but a `-:` followed
  by KEY segments (`-: name: Rex`) is now the MIDDLE form and addresses the last keyless
  element (the old keyless-conversion reading of that one spelling is retired; the appends
  spell `- name: Rex` or `-:` with a block). Positions (bare integer segments) are RESERVED in
  flat paths: named keys and `-` only. A row whose head addresses an EXISTING key paves into
  the LAST same-key sibling (the mirror of the middle `-` rule); plain single-segment rows
  keep their duplicate-tolerant reading - rows are commands only when they carry a path.
- >
  **Indentation.** A flat row's continuation block indents to the columns the NESTED spelling
  would give it in the same document - the number of indent steps equals the path's depth, as
  if the fold were undone. Flattening is a pure line fold: unfolding a flat row moves no other
  line's column. The law is EMIT-STRICT, PARSE-TOLERANT: the serializer writes exactly those
  columns; the parser accepts any strictly deeper block as the row's continuation.
- >
  **The write-once constraint.** A scalar value may land on a node only while that node is
  null-valued AND childless. This is what makes flattening and de-flattening predictable and
  unambiguous - a path, once paved past, cannot be silently overwritten:
- !!<format: text/x-yamlover> |
  key1: key2: 12
  key1: key2: 13            # INVALID - key2 already holds 12
- !!<format: text/x-yamlover> |
  key1: key2: key3: 100     # paving made key1: key2 a null-valued node with a child
  key1: key2: 13            # INVALID - the value is null, but key3 is already under it
- >
  The [unified node](*::language:model)'s self-value is still reachable: spell the value row
  FIRST, then pave children into it (`k: 13` then `k: sub: x` - the scalar grows fields, the
  omni). What flat rows cannot spell stays nested - a self line authored among its fields, a
  bookmark or `!!<...>` tag on an intermediate node, comments between segments - the same
  silent, lossless fallback flow form takes to block form
  ([yamlover/structured](*::language:concretes:03-yamlover:00-yamlover_stream:00-yamlover_code:00-yamlover_structured)).
- >
  **Combinations.** The flat row's tail is the ordinary
  [pair](*::language:concretes:03-yamlover:00-yamlover_stream:00-yamlover_code:00-yamlover_structured:00-yamlover_key_value_pair)
  tail - after the path, the node's properties may precede the value. `&` BOOKMARKS chain
  their own colon paths; each bookmark runs to the next `&` or to the value, and the value is
  the trailing colon-less token. (Precisely: a chain's scope colons and its FIRST name always
  belong to it, each further colon-terminated portion extends it, and a lone `-` portion - the
  keyless-membership suffix - belongs too; a `:`-led value token like `::: host` is always a
  VALUE.) The chain spellings are PARSE-ONLY tolerance: the serializer keeps the own-line
  canonical form, as it always has for bookmarks. The continuation block carries the rest of
  the node - further bookmark rows, fields, keyless elements - at the fold's computed depth:
- !!<format: text/x-yamlover> |
  normal: flat: key: &book: mark1: &another: book: mark: scalar
        &third: book: mark
        field1: value1
        - value2
- >
  **The pull link is exclusive.** A flat path may land a
  [reference](*::language:pointers:reference) as its value - and nothing else may share the
  node. A reference is an EDGE, not a node: in the model a pointer carries no bookmarks, no
  tag, no fields at all, so no `&` chain may precede it on the row and no continuation block
  may follow it:
- !!<format: text/x-yamlover> |
  normal: flat: key: *pull: link
- >
  **Escape.** Quotes make strings, as everywhere: a scalar that CONTAINS `: ` is spelled quoted
  (`key1: 'key2: value'`) - which has always been the serializer's canonical emission for such
  values; the bare spelling now reads as the path it looks like. The differences this opens
  with YAML are catalogued [with the other deliberate breaks](*::language:vs-yaml:differences:flattening).
